  "order": 9,
  "title": "if and else",
  "summary": "Run a block of code only when a test is True, and another block when it is not.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "branch",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "`if` lets the program choose. You write a test, a colon, and then the lines to run when the test is True, **indented by 4 spaces**:\n\n```\nif temp > 25:\n    print(\"Shorts today\")\n```\n\nThe indented lines are the **block**. When the test is False, Python jumps over the whole block.\n\n`else:` adds a block for when the test is False. Exactly one of the two blocks runs, never both.\n\nThe first line that is back at the left edge is no longer part of the if: it always runs. The **which way?** panel shows the test, its answer and the path Python took.",
    "analogy": "An if is a fork in a path with a signpost asking one question. Yes sends you down the left path, no down the right. Both paths meet again further on, and from there everyone walks the same road."
  },
